          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Are there eating habits that tend to make reflux worse?",
          "answer": "Yes. Based on the lifestyle guidance on eating with reflux, the habits that matter most are:\n\n- **Large meals.** A big meal stretches the stomach and puts more pressure on the valve at its top, so the same food eaten in smaller portions usually causes fewer symptoms.\n- **Lying down soon after eating.** A full stomach is harder to keep in place when you are horizontal. The change most often recommended is to leave several hours between your last meal and lying down. The source says this can help more than changing foods, especially if your symptoms mainly happen at night.\n- **Trigger foods.** Coffee, chocolate, alcohol, citrus, tomato, mint, fatty or fried foods, spicy foods and carbonated drinks often appear on lists for reflux. However, the guidance says there is weak evidence that cutting all of them helps, and strict avoidance is hard to keep up. The usual approach is to find out which foods affect you personally.\n- **Body weight.** If your weight is above the healthy range, weight reduction has the strongest support of any lifestyle measure in this guidance.\n\nPractical steps that follow from this:\n1. Eat smaller meals, and spread the same food across more of them.\n2. Leave a gap of several hours between eating and lying down, especially at bedtime.\n3. Try removing one suspected trigger at a time, and keep a note of what you ate and how you felt, so you can see which foods make a difference for you.\n\nThe passage on identifying individual triggers was cut off, so I can't give you the full method from the source. Also, this guidance is general. If your reflux is frequent, is getting worse, or does not improve with these changes, talk to a clinician. If you have severe chest pain, trouble swallowing, vomiting blood, or black stools, seek urgent medical care straight away.\n\n*Source: Eating with reflux: meal size, timing, and finding individual triggers (lifestyle-gerd-diet).*\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
